  <title>What makes a red wine score well?</title>
  <dc:creator>Raeesa </dc:creator>
  <link>https://muvisu.github.io/Applying-biplotEZ/applications/wine-quality/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<section id="the-data" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-data">The data</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/186/wine+quality">wine quality data set</a> was collected by Cortez and colleagues in 2009. It records eleven physicochemical measurements for 1 599 red <em>vinho verde</em> wines from the north of Portugal, such as acidity, sugar, alcohol and sulphur dioxide, together with a quality score from 0 to 10 awarded by a panel of tasters.</p>
<p>Each row is one wine. The eleven measurements are the variables we will draw as axes. The quality score is used only to colour the points, so we can see whether chemistry and taste agree.</p>
</section>
<section id="the-question" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-question">The question</h2>
<p>Wine chemistry is measured in a lab. Wine quality is judged by people. Do the wines that people like share a chemical profile, and if so, which measurements matter?</p>
</section>
