<section id="what-the-picture-tells-us" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="what-the-picture-tells-us">What the picture tells us</h2>
<p>Because the eleven measurements are on very different scales (alcohol in percent, sulphur dioxide in milligrams per litre), each variable was standardised before the biplot was built. The plot then shows the two directions in which the wines vary most.</p>
<p><strong>Axes that point the same way measure the same thing.</strong> Fixed acidity and citric acid run almost parallel, and pH points the opposite way. That is the chemistry being explained: more acid means lower pH. Density and chlorides also travel together, pointing away from alcohol, because alcohol is lighter than water. Similarly, free and total sulphur dioxide point together because one is a component of the other.</p>
<p><strong>High-scoring wines sit on one side.</strong> The green points drift towards the alcohol axis and away from the volatile acidity axis. Volatile acidity is essentially vinegar, so wines with more of it score lower, while wines with more alcohol and more sulphates tend to score higher. The low-scoring orange points lean the other way.</p>
<p><strong>Chemistry only partly explains taste.</strong> The bands overlap heavily. Chemistry pushes a wine in a direction, but the tasting panel clearly responds to things the lab does not measure.</p>
</section>
<section id="take-aways" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="take-aways">Take-aways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Alcohol and sulphates are associated with higher scores; volatile acidity with lower scores.</li>
<li>Several measurements are near duplicates of each other, so the eleven variables carry far fewer than eleven separate pieces of information.</li>
<li>A biplot puts all of this in one picture: the groupings of wines, the relationships between measurements, and the link between the two.</li>
</ul>
